  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe forecast area remains masked beneath slow moving showers and\nembedded thunderstorms this afternoon. These showers, with \ncloud tops plateauing around 15-20kft AGL, have little steering\nflow aloft to help speed things along and has resulted in very\nlocalized flash flooding thus far today. Rain has fallen over\nthe same locations for several hours now, primarily across \nsouthwestern and north-central West Virginia, with only meager\nobservations capturing these higher rainfall accumulations. Some\nroad closures have been reported throughout the day, and the\nrisk for flash flooding extends into this evening. Therefore,\nwill maintain the ongoing Flood Watch through its forecast\nexpiration of 10 PM tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHeading into Thursday, precipitation coverage gradually\ndiminishes from north to south amid building high pressure from\nthe north and west. Simultaneously, upper level troughing will\nshift eastward and a strong coastal low will slither up the\neastern seaboard, which will keep the focus of showers and\nstorms to the east of the Central Appalachians. \n\nEstablishing high pressure and upper level ridging will then set\nthe stage for an extended period of dry weather from Friday \nthrough at least Monday. Next chance for rain still looks to be \naround the middle of next week.\n\nCooler air advecting into the region beginning Thursday night \ncould promote patchy frost over the higher ridges of Randolph \nand Pocahontas Counties. This was included in this afternoon's\nforecast package as a result. Otherwise, expecting Chamber of\nCommerce weather through this upcoming weekend into the start of\nnext week, with daytime highs reaching the 60s/70s, and\novernight low will feel crisp around the 40s/50s.",
